Here in the United States, today (Thursday) is Thanksgiving. It was originally a communal event to give thanks for the year’s bounty. These days, it’s a family affair where the—sometimes extended—family gets together to share a meal. Traditionally, that meal is a roast turkey
</p>
<p>
That’s what I did today. Generations of turkey eaters claim that the tryptophan in turkey makes them sleepy so it’s also traditional to take a nap after the meal. It turns out that there’s not enough tryptophan in a turkey to make you sleepy and more likely the high carbohydrate meal and probably accompanying wine is what’s responsible for the sleepiness.
</p>
<p>
Truth to tell, we here at the Irreal bunker don’t care that much for turkey so we had something else but it was still a rich enough meal to make me lethargic. All this is to say that I didn’t feel up to researching and writing a blog post today so you get this note instead. But at least you know the truth about the tryptophan fable now.</p>
